{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' solver_index.md; if test -f frontier_brief.md; then sed -n '1,240p' frontier_brief.md; fi\"","aggregated_output":"# Generated solver source index\n\nUse line ranges to inspect only definitions relevant to the current level.\n\n## players.py\n- L5--13 `def play_level_1(env):`; calls: move_until_level_progress, prime_board, select_grid_cells_of_color\n- L16--23 `def play_level_2(env):`; calls: move_until_level_progress, select_grid_cells_of_color\n\n## legs.py\n- L5--7 `def prime_board(env, action=1):` — Spend the board's initial inert input so subsequent controls take effect.\n- L10--20 `def select_grid_cells_of_color(env, xs, ys, color, click_action=6):` — Click the sampled cells of a coordinate grid that currently match color.; calls: int\n- L23--29 `def move_until_level_progress(env, action, max_steps):` — Repeat a movement while bounded, stopping as soon as the level advances.; calls: range\n\n## perception.py\n- L23--36 `class Blob:`; calls: dataclass\n- L39--40 `def arr(frame) -> np.ndarray:`\n- L43--45 `def color_counts(frame) -> Dict[int, int]:`; calls: arr, int, zip\n- L48--79 `def connected_components(frame, colors: Optional[Iterable[int]] = None, min_area: int = 1) -> List[Blob]:`; calls: Blob, arr, int, len, max, min, range, sorted, sum\n- L82--89 `def block_signatures(frame, cell: int = 4) -> Dict[Tuple[int, int], Tuple[int, ...]]:` — Partition a frame into fixed cells and return each cell's color signature.; calls: arr, int, range, sorted, tuple\n- L92--110 `def object_candidates(frame, cell: int = 4, min_area: int = 4) -> List[dict]:` — A compact, game-agnostic object list from color components and cell signatures.; calls: arr, block_signatures, connected_components\n- L113--123 `def frame_delta(before, after) -> dict:`; calls: arr, int, len, zip\n- L126--133 `def action_deltas(env, actions: Sequence[int] = ACTIONS) -> Dict[int, dict]:`; calls: arr, frame_delta, int\n- L136--142 `def replay(env, actions: Sequence[int]):`; calls: int\n- L145--153 `def path_result(env, actions: Sequence[int]) -> dict:`; calls: bool, color_counts, int, len, object_candidates, replay\n- L156--161 `def changed_signature(env, actions: Sequence[int], cell: int = 4):`; calls: block_signatures, replay, set, sorted\n- L164--186 `def bounded_bfs(env, goal_fn, actions: Sequence[int] = (UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, USE), key_fn=None, max_states: int = 20000, max_depth: int = 80):` — Generic clone BFS over observational keys. Use small max_states first.; calls: arr, deque, goal_fn, int, key_fn, len\n- L189--229 `def bounded_replay_bfs(env, goal_fn, action_fn, key_fn=None, max_states: int = 20000, max_depth: int = 80):` — Path-only BFS for games whose deep Arena clones become expensive.; calls: action_fn, arr, deque, goal_fn, int, isinstance, key_fn, len, reconstruct\n- L232--233 `def level_goal(base_level: int):`\n\n## solve.py\n- L3--13 `def solve(env): # dispatch to the per-level player for the current level, in a loop`; calls: fn, getattr\n# Unverified frontier brief: sc25 level 3\n\nThis is a compact index of the latest clean WIP, not solver evidence.\nReproduce every observation you rely on with the documented local API.\nDo not reread the full proposer transcript unless a named ambiguity requires it.\n\n## Prior proposer progress\n\n- I’ll stay inside this workspace and use only the documented harness/perception surface. I’ll first read the index and prior frontier brief if present, then run the baseline and probe level 3 with compact symbolic summaries before editing the leg library and player.\n- The baseline is stable: the current solver reaches level 3 (`levels=2`, 22 moves). I’m now reproducing the frontier’s level-3 observations from the preserved compact probe, especially avatar controls, the coordinate panel, barrier/exit geometry, and whether action 6 has a context-dependent effect.\n- The compact frame resolves the geometry: the 9/10 avatar starts in the upper playfield; actions 1/2/3/4 are up/down/left/right. A color-13 barrier blocks the route to the matching 9/10 exit, while a color-4 obstacle blocks access to a color-6-ring/color-13 device on the right. The lower 3×3 panel is a local coordinate grid, and its separate clue shows three color-6 marks in the middle column. I’m probing panel state cycles and interactions at both obstacles now.\n- The panel clue is now verified: selecting its three middle-column cells causes the marked panel to reset to a uniform state, which is a real accepted-pattern transition rather than three independent toggles. The right-side obstacle can be bypassed only by moving fully above or below it; my first route was one row too close. I’m correcting that route and testing whether contact with the enclosed color-13 device changes the barrier’s latent state, then returning to the exit in the same clone.\n- A stronger interpretation emerged from the accepted-pattern reset: the panel behaves like a reusable command pad. Completing the clue once clears the marks, so a single submission may only advance a hidden mechanism by one unit. I’m testing repeated submissions of the verified middle-column pattern and measuring barrier/device positions after each round, using their displacement/removal as dense progress before checking the exit.\n\n## Preserved local probes\n\n- `probe_level2.py` (7657 bytes)\n- `probe_level2_interactions.py` (4048 bytes)\n- `probe_level3.py` (4545 bytes)\n- `probe_level3_compact.py` (3022 bytes)\n- `probe_level3_device.py` (2400 bytes)\n- `probe_level3_push.py` (2331 bytes)\n- `probe_level3_repeat.py` (1189 bytes)\n\nRun or inspect the smallest relevant probe before writing another one.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
